  "modelingNotes": [
    "New York Hall of Science, Flushing Meadows–Corona Park — original procedural geometry, CC0-1.0. bun generators/new-york-hall-of-science.ts",
    "Map frame: x east, y north, z up, metres. Bearing 0, so x/y are true east/north. Origin is the centroid of OSM way 284860788 (40.7472904, -73.8516846); that single outline (31.5 m, no building:parts) is the whole complex, so the model covers all of it:",
    "the 1964 Great Hall (Harrison & Abramovitz): an undulating, lobed concrete drum, 31.5 m tall, its walls a grid of cobalt dalle-de-verre panels, drawn as a few broad rows of recessed blue cells;",
    "the later wings, plain: a low brick podium over the rest of the outline, the brick entrance rotunda on 111th Street and the sloped 2004 north wing;",
    "Rocket Park to the north-west: the Mercury-Atlas and Gemini-Titan II, at their OSM positions, as simple white rockets.",
    "The Great Hall's plan (about 29 x 42 m) is taken from aerial imagery, registered to OSM on the entrance rotunda.",
    "Original procedural model from https://github.com/alexwohlbruck/landmarks (catalog id \"new-york-hall-of-science\", model \"new-york-hall-of-science\"), generated by https://github.com/alexwohlbruck/landmarks/blob/c3921f733df14b41e1f6d15853cce7b343886f17/generators/new-york-hall-of-science.ts. That repository is the editable source; source.blend.gz is the exported mesh imported into Blender for convenience.",
    "Exported for Open Landmarks v1: placement bearing and scale baked in; moving parts frozen in their rest pose; footings and basements below the lowest ground (y = 0) cut away; material colours carried over exactly, as library finishes where they are not standard swatches.",
    "To fit six materials, folded new-york-hall-of-science-great-hall-concrete into new-york-hall-of-science-roofs (ΔE 5.1), new-york-hall-of-science-rocket-white into new-york-hall-of-science-pale-metal (ΔE 9.3)."
  ],
